  "body": "`Search-Forge`, `Invoke-ForgeApi`, and `Get-ForgeConfiguration` map onto commands both providers already ship, so the work was in the vocabulary rather than the dispatch.\r\n\r\n`Invoke-ForgeApi` matters beyond its own convenience. The unified surface wraps a fraction of what the two providers expose, and without an escape hatch the answer to anything unwrapped is \"ForgeCli can't\". With one, it is \"ForgeCli doesn't wrap that yet, here is the raw call\" — the response is the provider's own shape, which is the honest trade for reaching an endpoint nobody has unified.\r\n\r\n`Search-Forge -Scope` needed a forge vocabulary rather than a passthrough: GitHub's `code` and GitLab's `blobs` name the same thing. `code` and `repos` work on both; the rest are one-sided, and a scope the active provider cannot express warns and searches code instead of failing. Once a caller writes `-Scope code` the accepted set is public API, so it is settled here rather than grown as providers reveal values.\r\n\r\n`Get-ForgeConfiguration` returns the active provider's configuration and nothing more. ForgeCli's own settings — the clone root and layout issue #6 needs — belong to a separate command, so neither redefines the other.\r\n\r\nRefs: https://github.com/chris-peterson/pwsh-forge/issues/10",
